## Decoding the question
A **10-mark note** (about **550–650 words**). The SDGs have **never been asked** as a descriptive question, though MCQs asked which SDG targets gender equality (2026). State the goal and its **targets**, then measure **Pakistan's progress** with dated data, target by target, and end with a judgement.

## The model answer

### 1. SDG 5: the goal

#### i) The goal
**SDG 5** — "**Achieve gender equality and empower all women and girls**" — is one of the 17 Sustainable Development Goals adopted by the UN in ==2015== for the period to ==2030==.

#### ii) Its origins
It succeeded **MDG 3** (gender equality and women's empowerment) and carries forward the commitments of **CEDAW** (==1979==) and the **Beijing Platform for Action** (==1995==).

#### iii) A cross-cutting goal
Gender targets also run through the other goals — poverty, health (SDG 3), education (SDG 4), decent work (SDG 8) and peace and justice (SDG 16) — so SDG 5 is both a goal and a lens on all the others.

### 2. The nine targets

#### i) Rights and violence
**5.1** end discrimination; **5.2** eliminate violence, including trafficking; **5.3** eliminate **child, early and forced marriage** and female genital mutilation.

#### ii) Work and power
**5.4** recognise and value **unpaid care** and domestic work; **5.5** ensure women's full participation and **equal leadership**; **5.6** universal access to **sexual and reproductive health**.

#### iii) Means of implementation
**5.a** equal rights to **economic resources, land and property**; **5.b** use **technology** to empower women; **5.c** adopt and strengthen **policies and laws** for gender equality.

### 3. Pakistan's commitment

#### i) The 2016 resolution
In **February 2016** the National Assembly unanimously adopted the SDGs as Pakistan's **national development agenda** — one of the first parliaments to do so.

#### ii) The machinery
**SDG support units** were set up federally and in the provinces to track progress.

### 4. Progress: laws and policies

::: fig mapping | How SDG 5 targets connect to Pakistani laws and programmes.
@left SDG 5 target
@right Pakistani response
5.2 Violence => Harassment Act 2010; honour law 2016; Anti-Rape Act 2021
5.3 Child marriage => Sindh 2013; Islamabad 2025 (age 18)
5.4 Unpaid care => BISP cash to women; time-use data
5.5 Leadership => 60 NA reserved seats; Elections Act 2017
5.a Property => Women's Property Rights Act 2020
5.b Technology => Digital inclusion; mobile gap down to 25%
:::

#### i) Violence (5.2)
The workplace harassment law (==2010==), the honour-killing law (==2016==), the **Anti-Rape (Investigation and Trial) Act 2021** and domestic-violence laws in every province and Islamabad (==2013–2025==).

#### ii) Child marriage (5.3)
Marriage at **18** in **Sindh** (==2013==) and **Islamabad** (==2025==), and a Punjab bill passed in **April 2026**.

#### iii) Leadership and property (5.5, 5.a)
**60** reserved National Assembly seats, the **Elections Act 2017** (5% tickets; 10% turnout rule) and the **Enforcement of Women's Property Rights Act 2020**.

### 5. Progress: outcomes and data

#### i) The overall picture
Pakistan ranked ==143rd of 145== in the Global Gender Gap Report ==2026==, up from last (==148th of 148==) in ==2025==.

#### ii) Work and care
Female labour-force participation is **24.4%** (LFS ==2024–25==); unpaid care remains uncounted in national accounts.

#### iii) Health
The maternal mortality ratio was **186** per 100,000 live births (PMMS ==2019==), far above the SDG 3.1 target of below 70.

#### iv) Leadership
Only **11** women won general National Assembly seats in ==2024==, and there were no women federal ministers in ==2025==.

#### v) Education
Female literacy is about **53%** (Census ==2023==), and more than **25 million** children are out of school, most of them girls — a gap that drags on every other target.

#### vi) Technology
The **mobile-internet gender gap** fell from **38%** to **25%** (GSMA, ==2024–25==) — one of the clearest gains.

### 6. Assessment

#### i) Strong on paper
Pakistan has adopted laws and policies for most targets — a record that is strong on **5.c**.

#### ii) Slow in outcomes
Outcomes — work, health, leadership, violence — are changing slowly; enforcement, funding and provincial capacity after the **18th Amendment** are the main constraints.

#### iii) The lesson of the MDGs
Pakistan **missed most MDG targets**, including those on gender parity in education and maternal mortality — a warning that commitments without enforcement and funding repeat the same result.

#### iv) The verdict
At the current pace Pakistan will **not** achieve SDG 5 by 2030. Faster progress needs enforcement of existing laws, investment in girls' education and maternal health, childcare and safe transport for women's work, and sex-disaggregated data to track every target.

## Why this answer scores
- States the goal and **all nine targets**, grouped for clarity.
- Records Pakistan's **February 2016** parliamentary commitment.
- Measures progress **target by target**, with laws and **dated data** (GGGR 2026, LFS 2024–25, PMMS 2019, GSMA).
- A **mapping** figure links targets to Pakistani laws; the verdict is clear.
